Link Sonny disposition: Williams was all smiles at the Sydney Fish Markets on Friday afternoon. Photo: Steven Siewert
The Roosters chairman was on his way to the airport before leaving town on business, but he had no idea if his most prized possession - in a footballing sense - was staying for another season or hightailing it to rugby union. ''I had no idea what he was doing,'' Politis told Fairfax Media.
Earlier that day, video emerged of Williams locked in discussions with representatives from the New Zealand Rugby Union and Chiefs coach Dave Rennie outside a restaurant in Brighton-le-Sands. ''Even then I thought, 'What will be will be','' Politis said.
Then came the phone call from Williams himself that he had snubbed the All Blacks jumper for another year, and will now attempt to win consecutive premierships wearing the Tricolours for one more season.
''It's wonderful,'' said Politis, who is publicity shy, even at these moments. ''What more is there to say?''
Advertisement
That Politis didn't know if Williams was staying or going furthers the notion that the 28-year-old was undecided about his future until the past few days. Indeed, there was a strong belief among some Roosters in the lead-up to the grand final that he would be joining the Chiefs in the Super Rugby competition next year.
Williams won a World Cup with the All Blacks on home soil in 2011 and one of his closely held ambitions is ensuring his country wins the title outside New Zealand for the first time since 1987. With the 2015 Rugby World Cup to be played in England, he had said last week that an extra year of rugby might be the way to prepare for that challenge.
Winning the grand final with the Roosters changed all of that. Those close to him reported mid-year that Williams was gone. The deeper the Roosters went into the season, and closer to the finals, the more he bonded with his new teammates, especially the Polynesian players.
For all the brickbats thrown at his head about being a mercenary and selfish player, his teammates are effectively kin. When he learnt Roosters ''brothers'' Sam Moa and Frank-Paul Nu'uausala were playing for the Kiwis instead of Tonga or Samoa respectively at the Rugby League World Cup, Williams backflipped on his decision to shun the tournament.
Similarly, winning a premiership with the Roosters sealed his decision to stay for one more year. We all know what that means. Another year of Williams being painted as either hero or villain. As a pay-cheque player or player who is nothin' but heart, bra.
What will surprise you is that Williams hasn't necessarily enjoyed his days since winning the grand final. He's told others that he's felt ''flat''. The criticism has hurt. He has more than 300,000 followers on Twitter, and make no mistake: the grubby missives over 140 characters have stung him. In some respects, he doesn't help himself.
His change in thinking about playing for the Kiwis at the World Cup squeezed out young Storm back-rower Tohu Harris, and that is not palatable by any means.
But some of the ridiculous commentary around his ''secret meeting'' with NZRU officials at Brighton-le-Sands on Thursday captures how much he polarises opinion. Some saw it as a shameless and very public middle-finger salute to the NRL and the Roosters. And now? After he's signed on for one more year? Well, now it just looks like Williams enjoys alfresco dining.
