Farmers say they'll keep pushing for a mandatory code of conduct for dealings with supermarkets, as the Federal Government seeks comment on a proposed voluntary code.
The proposed Food and Grocery Code of Conduct is based on one drafted by Coles, Woolworths and the Australian Food and Grocery Council, and is voluntary, meaning a supermarket would have to agree to 'opt-in' to be bound by the code.
The code would be prescribed under the Competition and Consumer Act and the government says that means the Australian Competition and Consumer Commission (ACCC) could take enforcement action against parties not adhering to the code.
Peter Darley, an apple grower from New South Wales who has been chairing a national working group pushing for a mandatory code, says the voluntary code won't help farmers.
The code would allow retailers to ask suppliers to agree to exemptions that permit otherwise prohibited behaviour to occur, provided the retailer meets certain conditions.
"This is just another way of the supermarkets saying we're just going to do business the way we've always done business," Mr Darley said.
"It's an out for them. As far as I'm concerned, it's got to be mandatory.
"We've got to know exactly what we're being paid for our produce."
The National Farmers Federation's general manager of policy Tony Mahar says the federation will look at the draft code, but its policy favours a mandatory code.
"That's our policy position at the moment and until that changes after consultation with our members we will maintain that policy position," Mr Mahar said.
"The government is clearly looking for some feedback on the value and the merit of a voluntary code{r} of{r} conduct{r}.{r}
