Lincoln National Corp. wanted to sell its subsidiary, Chicago Title & Trust Co., so it could redeploy its assets in property-casualty insurance lines.
Alleghany Corp., which is little more than a shell with $333 million in cash after selling Investors Diversified Services Inc. (IDS) to American Express Co. in January, was seeking to buy an operating company, preferably one in financial services.
Lincoln`s investment counsel, Salomon Brothers of New York, played matchmaker in a $128 million deal that in many ways appears to be an ideal marriage.
When Alleghany`s acquisition of Chicago Title and its principal subsidiary, Chicago Title Insurance Co., is sealed Thursday, the New York-based investment firm will acquire an operation that has posted one of the title industry`s strongest growth rates of recent years.
In turn, Lincoln National of Ft. Wayne, Ind., will receive $60 million cash and a six-year, $68 million note bearing a market rate of interest.
As for Chicago Title`s present management, ''Alleghany has emphasized management continuity,'' said Richard P. Toft, president of Chicago Title & Trust and chairman of Chicago Title Insurance. (The parent company has been without a chairman since Alvin W. Long retired in 1982.)
Alleghany has a small staff, Toft said, ''and has given every indication it wanted to acquire a company fully capable of being autonomous.''
It`s quite a turnabout of events for Chicago Title, which only a few years ago was operating in the red.
Title insurance protects a building owner and mortgage lender against claims against a property. If a search of ownership records shows a title is
''free and clear'' of liens and claims, a title insurer issues a clean bill of health and a title-insurance policy.
If the insured title subsequently becomes clouded by unknown heirs, overlooked liens, incomplete or erroneous records or outright frauds, the insurer is obligated to reimburse a policyholder for losses and related legal costs.
Unlike life, health and property-casualty insurers, whose earnings are derived largely from renewal premiums, a title insurer derives the bulk of its revenues from the one-time sale of title policies. As a result, a title company`s profitability depends on how well the real estate market fares.
One need look no further for an example of this linkage than to Chicago Title`s performance during the housing downturn earlier this decade.
In 1980, Chicago Title & Trust posted the first operating loss in its 134-year history--$1.36 million--on total revenues of $230.15 million. In 1981, the loss grew to $9.56 million on revenues of $228.9 million, and in 1982, the deficit dropped to $4.56 million on revenues of $215.52 million.
However, with the housing recovery of 1983, the company earned $17.96 million on revenues of $313.97 million. In 1984, earnings soared to a record $23.12 million on revenues of $342.83 million.
With Chicago Title back on its feet, it was a propitious time for Lincoln to seek a buyer.
Richard L. Pollay, president of Chicago Title Insurance, explained what led to the losses and how the firm dealt with them.
In the late 1970s, ''we were completing the last phase of a national expansion at a time when the real estate market was peaking,'' Pollay said.
''We faced the start-up costs of new offices plus inflation and a reduction in (title policy) revenues simultaneously, something that had never happened before, not even during the Depression.''
Chicago Title employed about 7,000 people in 290 offices nationwide in 1979. While growing from a Midwest to a national firm, a course followed by many title insurers in the `70s, ''we had somewhat outrun our resources,''
Pollay said.
The company halved its payroll to 3,572 employees, of whom 1,163 are in the Chicago area; reduced the number of offices to 150; and consolidated its geographic divisions to 7 regions from 3 regions and 14 subregions.
To replace closed offices, the firm enlarged its network of independent agents, which number 3,600.
Nationally, Chicago Title is a strong No. 2 behind Los Angeles-based Ticor Group of mortgage insurance companies.
Chicago Title posted gross title fees of $283.64 million last year while Ticor posted $345.65 million, Pollay said.
Ranked behind Ticor and Chicago Title are Safeco Title Insurance Co., with gross fees of $245.89 million; Lawyers Title Insurance Co., $190.43 million; and Commonwealth Land Title Insurance Co., $183.5 million.
Chicago Title said it dominated Cook County last year with a 52 percent market share of title revenues. Distant runners-up included Intercounty Title Insurance Co., 16 percent; Ticor, 8.7 percent; Attorneys Title Guaranty Fund, 5.8 percent; and Safeco, 5.7 percent.
While insuring residential titles is expected to continue to be Chicago Title`s core business, the firm plans to expand its coverage of commercial and industrial properties, Toft said.
The company also is enlarging its trust department, which manages about $2 billion, said Vince Agnew, vice president of investments for Chicago Title & Trust.
The firm`s trust accounts have enjoyed an average return on investment of more than 15 percent a year during the last 10 years, Agnew said.
''There`s an effort to bring more assets under our management... and expand our activities by taking advantage of what has been an excellent, long- term investment record,'' Agnew said{r}.{g}
