When Syracuse's NBA franchise, the Nationals, was sold and moved to Philadelphia to become the 76ers in May of 1963, the team agreed to continue to play a couple of regular season games in Syracuse each season.
Fifty years ago today, on Feb. 28, 1967, the Sixers made their second, and final, appearance at the War Memorial that season.
Led by Wilt Chamberlain, Philadelphia were rolling with a 57-9 record, and on pace to break the league record of 62 wins in a regular season.
Their opponents that night were the Cincinnati Royals, led by their own superstar, Oscar Robertson. The Royals were struggling, 31-38, and third in the five-team Eastern Division.
The previous Sixer game in Syracuse that season had seen 7,723 people attend, although that game featured Detroit rookie, and Syracuse University great, Dave Bing.
War Memorial officials were optimistic before the game that the Wilt vs. Oscar matchup could draw 6,000 people. The game would be preceded by a Canastota-Morrisville Clark League game, and halftime entertainment was the Dundee Shamrocks, a team of boys who perform a ball-handling show.
Several former Nats figured to play in the game for both sides. The Royals had Len Chappell and Connie Dierking. The 76ers had Chet Walker, Dave Gambee, and Hal Greer. (Larry Costello missed out with a leg injury.)
An interesting side note to the game was Wilt Chamberlain was on the verge of breaking one of his own records. Entering the game Chamberlain had made his last 31 field goal attempts. One more would equal the record.
For Post-Standard's sports editor, Billy Reddy, the record was a double-edged sword. In his "Keeping Posted" column the morning of the game, Reddy wrote, "Wilt is becoming too interested in another figure. It can mean that Chamberlain is taking nothing but the'sure' shot, the close-in dunker that he can't miss."
Reddy called Robertson the NBA's most "complete" player.
Despite snowy weather which made driving hazardous, more than 7,400 people attended the game.
Chamberlain made his first four shots to bring the record to 35 consecutive made field goals, a record that still stands.{r}
