After several rounds of tweaking, a plan that will allow Old Town residents to replace aging mobile homes in the historic district with new ones received final, unanimous approval from Bluffton Town Council Tuesday.
Under the new ordinance, any replacement plans would be subject to review and approval from town staff. However, the burden on mobile home owners would be eased by exempting them from the architectural review process other homes go through. The plan will affect 58 mobile homes, according to town officials.
The issue gained traction in March, when council directed staff to determine factors that prevented the replacement of existing mobile homes in Old Town.
Staff eventually found that the homes did not meet design requirements posed in town code and that the use of nontraditional building materials did not meet the architectural guidelines set, town planner Erin Schumacher said, adding that the required modifications created a financial hardship for some homeowners and obstacles to others with heirs' property.
In June, the town's Planning Commission recommended approving the proposal with the condition that the mobile home owners go through review from the town Historic Preservation Commission (HPC) as well. Council struck that provision down in July and remanded it back to the Planning Commission, which eventually agreed that the home owners could bypass the HPC, provided that all application requirements be met. Otherwise, replacement plans would have to undergo the full historical review process, Schumacher said.
Councilman Larry Toomer said the final approved plan is a good way to provide aid to longtime Bluffton residents.
"I would think anything they would replace would be a definite upgrade," Toomer said. "A lot of the people this will affect just aren't able to build $150,000 and $200,000 homes. They've been here in Bluffton a long time, and anything we can do to help them out, I'm all for it."
But Councilman Ted Huffman, despite his affirmative vote, worried that the ordinance does not have specific measures for qualifying the homes for replacement. Under the ordinance language, the 58 homes only have to be owner-occupied to qualify.
"This is a regressive ordinance," Huffman said. "We should have exemption cases for hardships, but with everything we're trying to do here, improving properties in Old Town, there needs to be some sort of qualification method in place."
Huffman also said a property owner would technically be able to circumvent the process by replacing a home and then renting it out. He suggested the mobile home owners be referred to the town's Affordable Housing Committee, which is spearheading the Bluffton Small Home Series to see if they would qualify for that program or the town's home repair program.
Mayor Lisa Sulka said that educating residents about the replacement process would be critical and that the ordinance could be changed, depending on the demand the town receives of property owners wanting to replace their homes.
In other business, council:
• Gave final approval of an amendment to the town's budget for this fiscal year that will allocate $89,000 toward the purchase of 21 security cameras to be placed throughout Old Town and as much as $6,000 for associated costs. Bluffton police say the cameras will be a public safety asset. They will be paid for by hospitality tax proceeds.
• Gave final approval of an amendment to town code that allows mobile ice cream vendors to now operate within town limits.
• Approved the town's Accommodations Tax Advisory Committee's recommendations to distribute the town's available proceeds from{g} the{g} Acc{r}ommod{r}ations{r} Tax{g}.{g}
