The federal government is entering into ��preliminary discussions�� with Quebec��s Davie shipyard to determine whether to ask the shipyard to retrofit a stop-gap supply ship for the navy.
��These discussions with Chantier Davie will help determine if it can provide an interim solution at a cost, time, and level of capability acceptable to Canada and he Royal Canadian Navy,�� according to a press release. ��Should the Government of Canada decide to pursue a provision of service contract agreement, it would provide the required standard of service to bridge the gap until the first Joint Support Ship is anticipated to be operational in 2021.��
Without a contract to do the work, roughly 200 jobs in Levis, Quebec — the riding of Public Safety Minister Steven Blaney — are expected to be cut, Davie has said.
When asked during a news conference what this announcement signals about the progress being made towards obtaining the two permanent vessels under the National Shipbuilding Procurement Strategy, Kenney said the project overall was still a success and stressed the developments made at Vancouver��s Seaspan and Halifax��s Irving shipyards as a result of the government��s $35-billion commitment to the project.
