SkyWest is out, Simplicity is in. And nearly 650 Denver airport workers are now looking for new jobs.
United Airlines has ended its contract with SkyWest Airlines for below-wing ground baggage handling at Denver International Airport.
SkyWest has held the contract for nine years. It is evaluated every three years, spokeswoman Marissa Snow said.
Chief operating officer Mike Thompson on Friday told employees by e-mail that starting Dec. 4, United��s below-wing ground handling responsibilities will be taken over by Simplicity Ground, a subsidiary of United Kingdom-based Menzies.
The change, which affects nearly 650 SkyWest employees, also means SkyWest will no longer have any ground baggage handling and ramp presence at DIA. The change does not affect United Express routes operated by SkyWest out of DIA, Snow said.
United, which declined to answer questions, said in a statement: ��We have decided to transition some of our ground handling work at DIA to Simplicity USA. This change will allow us to run a more competitive operation.��
Thompson��s e-mail suggests money was not the deciding factor.
��We were cost-competitive but not able to produce the SkyWest-caliber product we would like to deliver,�� Thompson��s e-mail said. ��I know we have also been challenged with resources, and United still has some strategic decisions and resources to address there, but we��re hopeful that lasting improvements will be made as United solidifies their overall strategy on the ground at Denver.��
Snow explained the contracting process is competitive and said there were areas where SkyWest fell short.
��As far as performance, it is about on-time and reliability,�� she said. ��Summer is always challenging, but we made changes and improvements in recent months.��
Industry index Airline Quality Rating, with data compiled by researchers at Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University and Wichita State University, ranks airline quality on multiple criteria, one of which is mishandled baggage. According to the 2012 report, the most recent available, United had a mishandled-baggage rate of 3.87 per 1,000 passengers, up from 3.66 per 1,000 in 2011 and 3.4 per 1,000 in 2010. Additionally, United and SkyWest shared the top spot for the largest decline in overall AQR score for 2012.
The move is the latest of several personnel shifts made by United. In July, United Airlines ��reclaimed�� 175 jobs in Denver that were outsourced to SkyWest. In June, United announced about 800 flight attendants — including about 260 based in Denver — would have to switch bases in October in order to ��rebalance�� several of its locations.
Some of the SkyWest employees affected by the most recent layoff notice previously lost their jobs in July, when United took back the customer service positions.
��Our customer service agents also lost their contract to United a few months ago and a lot of them moved to ramp, and then this just happened to them,�� said a worker, who asked not to be named because the SkyWest employee manual threatens with termination any worker who speaks to the media.
Snow corroborated the worker��s account, saying ��some employees may have recently transferred�� to ramp duties after furlough. She did not specify how many.
She said the company��s focus is helping its employees move forward.
��Our priority is the 650 SkyWest people affected by the transition; they will be offered transfer opportunities or a one-year furlough, with additional opportunities currently being evaluated,�� she said.
SkyWest will work with the company��s Employee Assistance Program and the state of Colorado to help find positions for the displaced employees, according to Thompson��s e-mail.
The Colorado Department of Labor and Employment will offer workshops and assistance to help the displaced employees find work, get additional training and continue to receive health insurance benefits, CDLE spokesman Bill Thoennes said.
Yet, despite low statewide unemployment numbers and a robust Colorado economy, he says these workers may still face challenges.
